  Where an LSTM helps is with partial observability and noise \'97 specifically when velocity comes from noisy VIO\
  rather than ground truth. The critique is right to flag this: in the competition, velocity comes from onboard\
  estimation, not a motion capture system. The cheapest fix that directly addresses this is observation history\
  concatenation (stack last K=3\'965 observations \uc0\u8594  36\'9660D input, same MLP). This:\
  - Handles latency and estimation noise without LSTM training instability\
  - Is the architecture the critique recommends as the "simplest defensible choice"\
  - Costs 20 lines of code and adds no training complexity\
\
  A GRU/LSTM makes sense later as an extension when you add the camera feed (VQ2), where temporal context matters a\
   lot for tracking gates across frames. Not for the state-based phase.\
\
  My recommendation: Add history stacking now (quick), keep LSTM as an extension for the vision phase.\
\
  ---\
  Big picture: what the competition context changes\
                                                                                                                   \
  Reading the AI Grand Prix emails, there are two things that significantly affect priorities:\
                                                                                                                   \
  1. The control interface is RPYT, not RPMs. This is actually fine \'97 our architecture is policy \uc0\u8594  waypoint delta \u8594 \
   DSLPIDControl \uc0\u8594  RPMs, and the inner-loop can be swapped to output RPYT commands instead. The policy itself      \
  doesn't change.                                                                                                  \
                                                            \
  2. VQ1 opens in May \'97 5 weeks away. The competition provides a camera feed, not ground truth gate positions. For \
  VQ1 the gates are highlighted with high SNR (visual guidance aids active), so gate detection can be simple. But\
  you need some gate detection to replace the ground truth relative gate positions in the observation.             \
                                                            \
  Team Northlake Labs (the blog post) is taking the same gym-pybullet-drones approach and has already hit Phase 0: \
  100% single-gate at 14.85 m/s. Their key architectural insight worth stealing: velocity alignment reward (dot\
  product of velocity toward gate) instead of Euclidean distance. This prevents the oscillate-near-gate reward hack\
   and directly aligns incentives.                          \
\
